      "claim": "The maximum-likelihood estimator (MLE) is the value of parameter \\(\\theta\\) that maximizes the likelihood function, which is the product of the probability density functions (PDFs) of observed data points.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:21:00\u201300:21:32] - 'So we have, like, the usual knowledge of the maximum likelihood estimator, as we've written in the chat. This is the first way that you have thought that you could learn on statistics courses. So we simply compute maximum likelihood estimation. So we're trying to maximize so-called likelihood function. And the likelihood function, in this case, is simply a product of those distributions where we substitute our observations in our density function.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "For independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) observations \\(x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n\\) from a distribution with unknown parameter \\(\\theta\\), the likelihood function is: \\(\\mathcal{L}(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i; \\theta)\\) where \\(f(x_i; \\theta)\\) is the PDF of the distribution at observation \\(x_i\\).",
